evision  screens will be isolated and you know as  I think one of your as Diana said in  Moscow you know Russians were saying we  don't want to be North Korea but you  know if they're not careful they the the  long-term effect on the Russian  government will be isolation  but it seems to me you know as I stand  here on the Ukrainian border and watch  these families coming across I saw a  family three generations coming across a  short time ago the grandmother in tears  you're you know reminding us all how  Britain must play by the rules  playing by the rules how far does he  have to go in breaking the rules before  we take some kind of direct action  well I think the question is how far  does Europe want us to get towards a war  across Europe  um with Russia how far does the British  public want us to go uh in the against a  nuclear armed power who may view  escalation of all means so I think we  have to be realistic here Ukraine and  I've said it even before this conflict  started Britain is at the Forefront of  helping try and get Ukraine into NATO it  takes all 30 countries to support it  we've had troops in Ukraine  through operational orbital since 2014  2015 helping the Ukrainian to train  we've trained 20 000 troops to get them  up to standard to join NATO we've done a  lot of that but others wanted to move at  a different pace so the reality is on  the day that Russia invaded Ukraine a  week ago  Ukraine was not in NATO so we have to do  everything else in our power to do that  but I'm afraid I'm not going to sit here  and say I'm going to put at risk the  rest of Europe by triggering a major  potentially War across the whole  continent  is the UK able to send planes to allow  ukrainians to enforce the no-fly zone  themselves  well look there isn't a no-fly zone so  let's just be really clear here and and  the other thing that no one seems to  talk about in the no-fly zone is who  does the no-fly zone benefit if you had  a no-fly zone in Ukraine the  overwhelming scale of the Russian army  would be able to drive around with  impunity which it can't at the moment  you talked about the armored column  without any air attack from the  ukrainians because there would be a  no-fly zone uh so it's a bit like in 19  you know the Battle of Britain if we  said oh let's have a no-fly zone of a  you know an unhelpful suggestion came  around saying let's not have a note  let's have a no-fly zone that would have  suited the vast you know German Vermont  Army uh we wouldn't have suited the  small uh you know the United Kingdom who  had just not far not previously just  returned back from Dunkirk so so you  know it let's let's ask ourselves what  would it have achieved you can still  have fighting with a no-fly zone it just  won't be in the air and that would allow  those Russian armored columns to drive  around with impunity and not be struck  from the air where currently uh  Ukrainian aircraft and Ukrainian uh  unmanned air vehicles are causing damage  on key parts of their logistical chain  so I think we should people who suggest  a no-fly zone should be really clear  what they're trying to achieve and how  it will be enforced because I don't see  them actually coming up with any logical  details behind it

